**Short Story:**  
Gnomish warlock Zizzik tinkered with his goggles as his Succubus, Sparklefizzle, fluttered ahead through Stranglethorn’s humid jungle. Spotting a hostile troll, Zizzik sent Sparklefizzle in with a hiss. She used *Lesser Invisibility* to vanish behind the troll, then *Lash of Pain* as he turned—shadowy tendrils searing his back. Before he could raise alarm, *Seduction* lulled him into a daze. Zizzik sprinted up, popping a healing potion, while Sparklefizzle dropped *Soothing Kiss*, dropping threat to zero. The troll slept on, blissfully unaware as Zizzik looted its rare herbs. "See, boss?" he cackled to his pet. "Stealth and subtlety beats brute force every time."

---

**Answer:**  
Warlocks favor Voidwalkers for group content and tanking due to their superior threat generation (*Torment*, *Suffering*) and utility (*Sacrifice* as emergency shields). While Succubus excels at solo stealth and CC, Voidwalkers offer reliable threat control in dungeons and raids, making them the "default" pet for utility in group scenarios—even if Succubus is better for solo questing. Classic WoW’s dungeon-heavy meta and warlock roles prioritize survivability and threat management, cementing Voidwalker as the go-to companion. [end of text]


